To understand the contribution of individual variables taking into account their nonlinear interactions, we develop a simple analysis approach using the joint tuning map, based on the intuition described above. For example, to quantify the contribution of variable $x$, we calculate the variability of the joint tuning map that is not explained by all other variables. In the case of two variables, this amounts to the residual variability that is not explained by $y$. Similarly, one can calculate the contribution of $y$ via the variability that is not explained by $x$. %Unlike analysis of variance (i.e., ANOVA) for linear additive models, summing up the contribution of $y$ and $x$ may not be equal to the total variance of the data. 

Practically, one complication in estimating the contribution of individual variables is measurement noise. Consider the case mentioned above in which the neural response is selective to $x$ while being completely invariant to $y$. Due to measurement noise (which could come from many sources), there will be residual variability in the estimated joint tuning maps that can not be explained by $x$. If one simply uses the residual variance to estimate the contribution of variable $y$, the true contribution will be over-estimated. To address this problem, we have developed a split-half procedure to estimate the variability induced by noise by quantifying the variability across the joint tuning estimated from the two equal splits of the data. Our final estimate for the contribution of individual stimulus variables removes such noise-induced variability (see Methods). 
%A schematic of our proposed analysis procedure is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:estimation}a. 
%Our mathematical analysis shows that the proposed estimator (details see the Method Section) is unbiased under the assumption that measurement noise is independent across the two halves. 
We perform systematic simulations to validate and benchmark our noise-corrected estimator based on split-half analysis. We find that the estimation procedure can well recover the relative contribution of each variable (for an example, see \textcolor{red}{Fig.~\ref{fig:estimation}d,e}). %However, there are also cases that the estimation error is more substantial given limited amount of data .

