Our approach can be broadly applied to study the joint tuning of individual neurons to multiple behavioral variables simultaneously (ideally 2 or 3), without making additional assumptions regarding how tuning of different variables interact (such as multiplicative interactions assumed in GLM). We further demonstrated this point by studying the joint MD and HD tuning in head direction cells to show that head direction cells are largely invariant to movement direction. Thus, it is unlikely that head direction cells would encode the movement direction signal required for path integration~\cite{Mcnaughton2006}. 
Similar techniques may be used to understand the selectivity of the place cells on linear tracks with respect to location, time or distance traveled. It was proposed that hippocampal neurons on linear tracks might encode location, time, or distance traveled or a combination of these~\cite{Eichenbaum2014time,Chen2024}. Although some progress has been made, it remains challenging to disentangle the contribution of these different factors because they are highly correlated in the experiments. Our approach provides a new way to estimate the contribution of these different factors in future research. 

Our results have implications for the study of mixed selectivity of neurons~\cite{Rigotti2013}, which has been reported in various brain areas in different tasks \textcolor{red}{[refs]}. First, from a methodological perspective, our techniques provide a new way to quantify mixed selectivity through the examination of joint tuning and the quantification of the contribution of individual stimulus variables. Second, our results based on the analysis of spatial tuning properties of the deep network models suggest that, in some cases, mixed selectivity may arise due to the difficulty in identifying the correct feature sets or stimulus variables. For example, in the deep network models examined here, many neurons in the first layer are oriented filters, thus they encode the orientation of edges. 
%Analysis of orientation selectivity of these units would lead to a clear interpretation of their response properties. 
However, when analyzing the spatial tuning properties of these units, they exhibit complex non-linear mixed selectivity for these spatial variables. In this case, nonlinear mixed selectivity arises due to the highly nonlinear mapping between the visual feature and the spatial variables (location and head direction). Only for particular combinations of location and head direction of the agent, an edge of certain orientation would appear in the receptive field of the model unit. Empirically observed mixed selectivity in neural systems has been proposed to carry certain functional advantage in coding \textcolor{red}{[refs]}. The discussion above suggests that mixed selectivity could also arise due to mis-identification of the features encoded. Extra care would be needed to interpret these response patterns that appear to be mixed selective. 

