This quantity is useful because there are cases that the (marginal) tuning of a variable is flat, but nonetheless, it is important for explaining the variability of the response due to its joint tuning together with some other variables. 
In the absence of noise, the contribution of $y$ can be estimated by  
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i=1}^m\sum_{j=1}^n (f_{i,j}-\overline{f}_{y_i})^2.
\label{eq:1}
\end{equation}

%\textcolor{red}{The following needs to be re-written}
This expression captures the variability in the firing rate map that is not explained by $y$ (as $\overline{f}_{y_i}$ is conditioned on  individual $x$), providing a quantification of all contributions of the variable $x$.

Practically, due to the presence of noise, proper cross-validation is needed. In particular, in the presence of measurement noise, Eq.~\ref{eq:1} can lead to an overestimation of the contribution of a given variable. Even if a variable $y$ does not have any explanation power of $f_{i,j}$,  due to noise, $\sum_{i=1}^m\sum_{j=1}^n (f_{i,j}-\overline{f}_{y_i})^2 $ will be non-zero. To address this problem, we developed cross-validated statistics to estimate the contribution of individual stimulus variables. In particular, we find that the following statistics provide an unbiased estimate of the variability of firing rates explained by the variable $x$, under mild assumptions. 
%Here we make the assumption that the estimated firing rate in bin is corrupted by additive noise (\textcolor{red}{double check if this is indeed needed}). The noise in each bin can be different. 

\begin{equation}
C_y
= \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{m_i}\sum_{j=1}^{n_j} [(f_{i,j}-\overline{f}_{y_j})^2+ (f^t_{i,j}-\overline{f}^t_{y_j})^2- (f^t_{i,j} -f_{i,j})^2+ (\overline{f}_{y_j}-\overline{f}^t_{y_j})^2].
\label{eq:2}
\end{equation}

Practically, we evenly partition the data into training and test sets for each bin of the joint firing rate map of $(x, y)$. The statistics above are computed based on a given split. By partitioning the data multiple times and averaging this statistics over the different ways of partitioning the data, the variance of the resulting estimator, denoted as $\overline{C_y}$, can be reduced. 
Here, $m_i$ could be different for different $i$ to capture the different number of bins after filtering out certain bins. Similarly, $n_j$ could be different for different $j$.


We can also compute the contribution of the other variable $x$ using a similar method. A ratio that summarizes the contributions of the two variables can also be computed. The method can be generalized to more than two variables. In this case, to estimate the contribution of a given stimulus variable, one would need to condition on all other variables. Practically, sufficient data would be needed in order to accurately estimate the contribution to response variability. 

